  "body": "# Fix bi-predicted 16x8/8x16 macroblocks in B slices\r\n\r\nFixes #3712.\r\n\r\n## The bug\r\n\r\n`GetInterBPred()` (`codec/decoder/core/src/rec_mb.cpp`) declares `pMCRefMem` outside\r\nthe partition loop, but advances it to the second partition from **inside** the\r\nreference-list loop, and never resets it between partitions:\r\n\r\n```c\r\nfor (int32_t i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {              // i = partition (0 = left/top, 1 = right/bottom)\r\n  for (int32_t listIdx = LIST_0; listIdx < LIST_A; ++listIdx) {\r\n    if (IS_DIR (iMBType, i, listIdx)) {\r\n      ...\r\n      if (i) { pMCRefMem.pDstY += 8; ... }     // once per LIST, never reset\r\n      BaseMC (pCtx, &pMCRefMem, listIdx, ...); // LIST_1 overwrites LIST_0's result\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor a bi-predicted partition this fails twice over:\r\n\r\n1. **`i == 0`** \u2014 the `BaseMC()` for `LIST_1` overwrites the `LIST_0` prediction already\r\n   sitting in `pMCRefMem`. The `listCount == 2` branch then redoes the `LIST_1` motion\r\n   compensation into `pTempMCRefMem` and calls `BiPrediction()`, which therefore averages\r\n   **LIST_1 with LIST_1**. The partition ends up as a plain LIST_1 prediction.\r\n\r\n2. **`i == 1`** \u2014 the `if (i)` offset is applied once per list instead of once per\r\n   partition, so the destination is advanced **twice** (+16 luma). The `LIST_1` motion\r\n   compensation and the combine step write **outside the macroblock**, into the\r\n   neighbour, and the partition keeps a plain LIST_0 prediction.\r\n\r\nBoth the `IS_INTER_16x8` and `IS_INTER_8x16` paths have this shape.\r\n\r\nA single wrong macroblock is enough to ruin the whole picture, because Intra_16x16\r\nDC and Vertical prediction copy from the left and top neighbours: the error travels\r\nalong the macroblock row via `I16_PRED_DC_L` and then straight down every column via\r\nIntra_16x16 Vertical. Reference B pictures carry it into later frames.\r\n\r\n## Worked example\r\n\r\nDisplay frame 32 of the `original.mp4` attached to #3712 \u2014 MB(1,0), `mbtype = 0xf020`\r\n(`MB_TYPE_8x16 | MB_TYPE_L0 | MB_TYPE_L1`, i.e. B_Bi_8x16), both partitions bi-predicted\r\nwith `refidx = 0/0`:\r\n\r\n| partition | L0-only pred | L1-only pred | correct average | openh264 produced |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| left 8x16  | 191 | 174 | **183** | 174 (= L1 only) |\r\n| right 8x16 | 191 | 174 | **183** | 191 (= L0 only) |\r\n\r\nThe residual is identical in openh264 and in the reference decoders, so the\r\ndiscrepancy is purely in the prediction. Of the 1530 macroblocks in that frame only\r\n**2** were genuinely mis-predicted; the other 754 broken macroblocks were intra\r\nmacroblocks reproducing the error through DC/Vertical prediction.\r\n\r\n## The fix\r\n\r\nDerive each partition's destination from a saved macroblock base, send the first list\r\nof a partition to `sRef` and the second to `sTempRef` so the second `BaseMC()` cannot\r\nclobber the first, and combine once after the list loop.\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\nThe sample attached to #3712 (720x540, High profile, CABAC, B frames,\r\n`weighted_bipred_idc = 2`), 60 frames, compared against two independent decoders:\r\n\r\n| | frames differing |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Microsoft Media Foundation vs Intel Quick Sync | **0 / 60** (references agree) |\r\n| openh264 master | 30 / 60 (worst 34.9 dB) |\r\n| **openh264 + this PR** | **2 / 60** (92.6 dB, 102.8 dB) |\r\n\r\nThe two remaining frames differ by \u00b11 on block edges only and are a separate\r\nB-slice deblocking boundary-strength issue, unrelated to this change.\r\n\r\n### In-tree test vectors\r\n\r\nAll 54 decodable vectors under `res/`, patched vs unpatched:\r\n\r\n* **46 unchanged.**\r\n* **`Cisco_Men_whisper_640x320_{CABAC,CAVLC}_Bframe_9.264` \u2014 fixed.** Before: 4 of 9\r\n  frames differ from the reference decoders (worst 60.8 dB / 59.8 dB). After:\r\n  **bit-exact with both**, which agree with each other. These two vectors are a\r\n  self-contained, in-tree demonstration of the bug.\r\n* **6 `VID_*_temporal_direct` vectors \u2014 output changes but they are still not\r\n  conformant.** Both before and after this change they differ from the reference\r\n  decoders on most frames, so they are affected by a **separate, larger bug in the\r\n  temporal direct path** that this PR does not address. Worst-case PSNR against Intel\r\n  Quick Sync moves as follows:\r\n\r\n  | vector | before | after |\r\n  |---|---|---|\r\n  | VID_1280x544_cabac_temporal_direct | 36.21 dB | 62.89 dB |\r\n  | VID_1280x544_cavlc_temporal_direct | 23.94 dB | 24.30 dB |\r\n  | VID_1280x720_cabac_temporal_direct | 48.27 dB | 52.95 dB |\r\n  | VID_1280x720_cavlc_temporal_direct | 39.02 dB | 39.07 dB |\r\n  | VID_1920x1080_cabac_temporal_direct | 41.49 dB | 80.90 dB |\r\n  | VID_1920x1080_cavlc_temporal_direct | 30.27 dB | 30.68 dB |\r\n\r\n  No vector gets worse. I am happy to open a separate issue for the temporal direct\r\n  problem.\r\n\r\n## Expected-hash updates\r\n\r\nThe hashes currently in `test/api/decoder_test.cpp` match the **unpatched** output for\r\nall 8 affected vectors, i.e. they were recorded from the current decoder rather than\r\nfrom a conformant reference. They are updated here to the new output.\r\n\r\n`test/api/thread_decoder_test.cpp` keeps a separate table for the same vectors. It is\r\n**deliberately not touched here**, because its expectations cannot currently be made\r\nmeaningful: the multi-threaded decoder is non-deterministic. Six runs of the same binary\r\non `res/VID_1280x544_cabac_temporal_direct.264` with a fixed thread count produced six\r\ndifferent digests, while `threads=1` is perfectly reproducible. Since `SetUp()` also picks\r\n`(rand() % 2) + 2` threads per run, no single expected hash can be stable for the affected\r\nvectors. This is a pre-existing problem unrelated to this PR, and I will report it\r\nseparately.\r\n\r\n`test/api/decode_api_test.cpp` compares two decode passes against each other rather than\r\nagainst fixed hashes, so it needs no change.\r\n\r\n## Notes\r\n\r\n* Style follows `build/astyle.cfg`: 2-space indent, no tabs, no trailing whitespace,\r\n  LF line endings, longest added line 115 columns.\r\n* No new warnings with `-Wall`.\r\n* This PR was created with assistance from Claude Opus 5.",
